# polkit -- the thing every GNOME component asks before doing anything
# privileged.
#
# gnome-control-center changes a user's account type through it, gnome-shell
# raises the authentication dialog through it, gnome-settings-daemon and
# flatpak's system helper both refuse to act without it. It is also the reason
# pkexec exists at all: a setuid helper that asks polkitd whether the caller may
# run a program as someone else.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env --
# versions.env is regenerated wholesale from the LFS book by
# tools/pin-versions.sh and a desktop pin placed there is destroyed by the next
# `make pin`.
SRC_URL=https://github.com/polkit-org/polkit/archive/126/polkit-126.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=2814a7281989f6baa9e57bd33bbc5e148827e2721ccef22aaf28ab2b376068e8
SRC_DIR=polkit-126

# ==============================================================================
# THE JAVASCRIPT ENGINE
# ==============================================================================
# There is no -Djs_engine option in this recipe and there is no mozjs anywhere
# near it, and that is a fact about polkit 126 rather than a decision taken
# here: upstream REMOVED the mozjs backend in 124, so duktape is the only
# engine 126 can be built with. meson.build asks
#
#     js_dep = dependency('duktape', version: '>= 2.2.0', required: false)
#     ...
#     js_dep = cc.find_library('duktape', has_headers: ['duktape.h'], required: true)
#
# -- pkg-config first, then a bare library probe. The duktape recipe writes the
# duktape.pc that upstream does not ship precisely so the first of those
# succeeds, which is also what enforces the >= 2.2.0 version check; the
# find_library fallback would happily accept a duktape too old for polkit's
# rules API.
#
# So: nothing here can pull in a second JavaScript engine, and the packaged
# duktape 2.7.0 is what evaluates /etc/polkit-1/rules.d.
#
# ==============================================================================
# THE LOGIND INTERFACE
# ==============================================================================
# -Dsession_tracking=elogind. The choices are logind, elogind and ConsoleKit,
# and the default is logind -- which resolves dependency('libsystemd').
#
# THE DEFAULT WOULD HAVE BUILT HERE, AND THAT IS THE TRAP. elogind's
# post-install.sh symlinks libsystemd.pc to libelogind.pc so that GNOME
# components asking for 'libsystemd' find it, so -Dsession_tracking=logind would
# have configured, compiled, linked against libelogind and reported "logind" in
# the summary. It is named explicitly so the recipe says which session tracker
# it is wired to rather than depending on a compatibility symlink to be right by
# accident.
#
# What it buys: polkitd asks logind which session a caller belongs to and
# whether that session is active and local. With ConsoleKit -- unpackaged and
# dead -- every check would fall back to "no session", and an authorization rule
# written for an active local user would never match. That failure is silent in
# the worst direction: it does not refuse loudly, it declines to recognise the
# subject.
#
# ==============================================================================
# -Dos_type=lfs, WHICH IS NOT COSMETIC
# ==============================================================================
# os_type picks which PAM stacks data/polkit-1.in includes. Duct's Linux-PAM
# package ships the LFS layout -- system-auth, system-account, system-password
# and system-session, one file per management group (see linux-pam/install.sh).
#
# With os_type unset, meson probes for /etc/redhat-release and friends, finds
# nothing, reports "Linux distribution autodetection failed" as a MESSAGE rather
# than an error, and falls through to the redhat default: system-auth for ALL
# FOUR groups. On Duct that produces an /etc/pam.d/polkit-1 whose account,
# password and session lines include a file containing only an `auth` rule --
# so PAM has no account or session module at all for polkit, and authentication
# fails at the moment a user is asked for their password. A build that prints a
# message and installs a broken PAM file is exactly the class this distribution
# keeps finding, so the distribution is named.
#
# -Dprivileged_group is left at its default, which with os_type != debian is
# `wheel`. duct-filesystem ships a wheel group, and 50-default.rules is
# generated to make it polkit's admin identity.
#
# -Dpam_prefix=/etc/pam.d, AND THIS IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A PRECAUTION.
# meson.build uses pam_prefix directly as an install_dir, defaulting it to
# `prefix / 'lib/pam.d'` -- so the first build of this recipe installed the
# generated PAM file to /usr/lib/pam.d, which is a real convention (Linux-PAM's
# vendor directory) and one this distribution does not use: linux-pam is built
# with no -Dvendordir, so libpam looks in /etc/pam.d and nowhere else. The file
# was present, correct, and never read. pkexec would then fall through to
# /etc/pam.d/other, which linux-pam's install.sh fills with pam_deny -- polkit
# denying every authorization on a system where nothing looks broken.
#
# Caught by the assertion in install.sh rather than by reading, which is the
# argument for asserting on the file's PATH and CONTENTS instead of on the build
# exiting 0.
#
# ==============================================================================
# THE REST
# ==============================================================================
# -Dauthfw=pam. Already the default; named because the alternative (`shadow`)
# would build a polkit that authenticates against /etc/shadow directly and
# therefore never runs pam_elogind -- so an authorization would not be attached
# to a logind session. Linux-PAM is packaged, so this is the right one.
#
# -Dintrospection=true. Already the default, and load-bearing: gnome-shell's
# polkit agent is JavaScript and reaches polkit only through the Polkit-1.0
# typelib. Named so that a future -Dintrospection=false is a decision rather
# than a default, and because the recipe check keys the introspection
# dependencies off this flag.
#
# -Dman=false. Also the default, and named because the trap is armed here:
# man=true renders through xsltproc and the DocBook stylesheets, libxslt IS
# packaged, and docbook-xml/docbook-xsl are not. Anyone turning man pages on
# would get a build that fails inside the XSLT rather than at configure.
#
# -Dtests=false: the suite needs dbusmock and dbus-python, neither packaged.
#
# NOT PASSED, all already false upstream: -Dexamples, -Dgtk_doc, -Dlibs-only.
# -Dgettext is also false by default, which controls only the ENABLE_GETTEXT
# define; po/meson.build calls i18n.gettext() unconditionally either way, which
# is why gettext is still a declared build dependency.
#
# SYSCONFDIR IS DELIBERATELY NOT PASSED. pk_pkgsysconfdir is
# get_option('sysconfdir') / 'polkit-1', and meson maps sysconfdir to the
# absolute /etc when the prefix is /usr -- measured for fuse3 and written up in
# its pkg.env. So /etc/polkit-1 lands in /etc, not in /usr/etc, without a flag.
#
# The systemd unit, sysusers.d and tmpfiles.d files ARE installed and are then
# removed by install.sh; see the argument there.
MESON_ARGS="-Dsession_tracking=elogind -Dauthfw=pam -Dos_type=lfs \
-Dpam_prefix=/etc/pam.d -Dintrospection=true -Dman=false -Dtests=false"
